<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Livingstone, Zambia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Livingstone is a town in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> River, close to the Victoria Falls, named after the explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">David Livingstone</a>. It grew up near the new railway bridge over the falls in the early 1900s, became the capital of North-Western Rhodesia in 1907 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a> from 1911, and lost that role when the government moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusaka">Lusaka</a> in 1935. Today it is a centre of tourism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/livingstone-zambia.jpg" alt="Livingstone, Zambia" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: YWAM Livingstone · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Livingstone.Zambia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
